  "output": "We are given: \"Charlie wants to sell beeswax candles. For every pound of beeswax, he can make 10 tapered candles. One pound of beeswax and the wicks cost $10.00 in supplies. If he sells each candle for $2.00 each, what is his net profit if he makes and sells 20 candles?\"\n\nWe need to find net profit. Net profit = revenue - cost.\n\nFirst, determine how many pounds of beeswax he needs to make 20 candles.\n\nHe makes 10 candles per pound of beeswax. So for 20 candles, he needs 20/10 = 2 pounds of beeswax.\n\nCost of beeswax and wicks: 1 pound costs $10.00. So for 2 pounds, cost = 2 * $10.00 = $20.00.\n\nRevenue: sells each candle for $2.00. 20 candles * $2.00 = $40.00.\n\nNet profit = Revenue - Cost = $40.00 - $20.00 = $20.00.\n\nSo net profit is $20.\n\nThe answer should be an integer: 20.\n\nWe need to end with: FINAL: 20\n\nBut let's double-check: \"net profit\" is the profit after subtracting costs. Yes.\n\nThus final answer: 20.</think>Charlie needs 20 candles, and he makes 10 candles per pound of beeswax, so he uses 2 pounds of beeswax. The cost for 2 pounds is 2 × $10.00 = $20.00. He sells 20 candles at $2.00 each, earning 20 × $2.00 = $40.00. Net profit is $40.00 - $20.00 = $20.00.\n\nFINAL: 20",
